{
  "title": "What Is a Variable?",
  "subtitle": "Storing Information in Memory",
  "topic": "2.1 • WHAT IS A VARIABLE?",
  "body": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px; width: 100%;\">\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 4px; width: 100%;\">\n    <div style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px #111111; padding: 4px 14px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 900; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;\">\n      2.1 • Storing Information\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 30px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      What Is a <hlt>Variable</hlt>?\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #222222; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 620px;\">\n      A variable allows your program to store data in memory under a label so you can retrieve and use it whenever needed.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"lesson-ref-card\">\n    <div class=\"lesson-ref-header\">\n      <span class=\"ref-badge\">PREVIOUS KNOWLEDGE</span>\n      <span class=\"lesson-ref-title\">Connecting to Earlier Concepts</span>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"lesson-ref-desc\">\n      Remember how computers store data in temporary RAM and how the print() command outputs values to the screen?\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;\">\n      <a href=\"1.html?spcl=1&lsn=1&sub=3&ref_from=1_4_1\" class=\"ref-jump-btn\">Review: Computer Memory & Storage (Lesson 1.2) →</a>\n      <a href=\"1.html?spcl=1&lsn=2&sub=3&ref_from=1_4_1\" class=\"ref-jump-btn\">Review: The print() Command (Lesson 2.2) →</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fde047; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 20px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Analogy A • The Labeled Box\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      A Box with a Label on the Outside\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Imagine a physical box. You write a label on the outside like <hlt>age</hlt>, and put a piece of paper reading <hlt>25</hlt> inside. Whenever you need that number, you ask Python for the contents of <hlt>age</hlt>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n      age = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">25</span><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(age)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Opens box 'age' and outputs: 25</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #93c5fd; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 20px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Analogy B • The Nickname\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Giving a Friendly Name to a Value\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Instead of retyping a piece of data over and over, you give it a short nickname. For example, storing <hlt>\"Kochi\"</hlt> under the nickname <hlt>city</hlt>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n      city = <span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"Kochi\"</span><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(city)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Outputs: Kochi</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fca5a5; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Rules & Common Pitfalls\n    </span>\n    <ul style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <li><strong>Left vs Right:</strong> The variable name ALWAYS goes on the left, value on the right: <hlt>age = 25</hlt> (never 25 = age).</li>\n      <li><strong>Quotes Matter:</strong> <hlt>print(age)</hlt> opens the box to show 25. <hlt>print(\"age\")</hlt> literally prints the word \"age\".</li>\n      <li><strong>Create Before Use:</strong> You cannot print a box before creating it, or Python will raise a NameError.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n</div>"
}